"We have the following system of equations:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n2d &= 17e - 8 \\\\\n2e &= d - 9\n\\end{align*}\n\nFirst, we can solve for $d$ in the second equation:\n\n$$d = 2e + 9$$\n\nNext, we substitute this expression for $d$ into the first equation:\n\n$$2(2e + 9) = 17e - 8$$\n\nExpanding and simplifying, we get:\n\n$$4e + 18 = 17e - 8$$\n\nSubtracting $4e$ from both sides, we get:\n\n$$18 = 13e - 8$$\n\nAdding 8 to both sides, we get:\n\n$$26 = 13e$$\n\nDividing both sides by 13, we get:\n\n$$e = \\frac{26}{13} = \\boxed{\\frac{26}{13}}$$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\frac{26}{13}$. I hope it is correct."
"First, let's determine the total amount of supplies needed for the original ten-day trip with twelve people. Each person gets a daily share, so the total amount of supplies required is:\n\n\\[ 12 \\text{ people} \\times 10 \\text{ days} = 120 \\text{ person-days} \\]\n\nNow, three more people join, making the total number of people fifteen now. We need to find out how many days the supplies will last with fifteen people. The total amount of supplies remains the same, so we set up the equation:\n\n\\[ 15 \\text{ people} \\times d \\text{ days} = 120 \\text{ person-days} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( d \\):\n\n\\[ d = \\frac{120 \\text{ person-days}}{15 \\text{ people}} = 8 \\text{ days} \\]\n\nTherefore, the supplies will last for \\(\\boxed{8}\\) days."
"First, we need to find the polynomial $g(x) = f(x + 1)$.\n\nGiven $f(x) = x^7 - 3x^3 + 2$, we substitute $x + 1$ into $x$:\n\n\\[g(x) = (x + 1)^7 - 3(x + 1)^3 + 2.\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the sum of the coefficients of $g(x)$. The sum of the coefficients of a polynomial is found by evaluating the polynomial at $x = 1$.\n\n\\[g(1) = (1 + 1)^7 - 3(1 + 1)^3 + 2.\\]\n\n\\[g(1) = 2^7 - 3 \\cdot 2^3 + 2.\\]\n\n\\[g(1) = 128 - 24 + 2.\\]\n\n\\[g(1) = 106.\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the coefficients of $g(x)$ is \\(\\boxed{106}\\)."
