"To evaluate \\((\\sqrt[3]{13})^6\\), we can use the properties of exponents. Specifically, we know that \\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\). Here, \\(a = \\sqrt[3]{13}\\), \\(m = 1\\), and \\(n = 6\\).\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n(\\sqrt[3]{13})^{6} = 13^{1 \\cdot 6} = 13^2\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to calculate \\(13^2\\):\n\\[\n13^2 = 13 \\times 13 = 169\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\((\\sqrt[3]{13})^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{169}\\)."
"First, we need to find the values of $\\sqrt{27}\\) and \\(\\sqrt{26}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{27} \\approx 5.196\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sqrt{26} \\approx 5.190\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the ceiling and floor of these values.\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{27}\\right\\rceil = \\left\\lceil 5.196 \\right\\rceil = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\sqrt{26}\\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor 5.190 \\right\\rfloor = 5\n\\]\n\nNow, we subtract the floor from the ceiling:\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\sqrt{27}\\right\\rceil - \\left\\lfloor\\sqrt{26}\\right\\rfloor = 6 - 5 = 1\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"
