"To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(6^3\\) and \\(3^6\\), we start by expressing both numbers in terms of their prime factors.\n\nFirst, express \\(6^3\\):\n\\[6^3 = (2 \\cdot 3^3)^3 = 2^3 \\cdot 3^9.\\]\n\nNext, express \\(3^6\\):\n\\[3^6 = 3^6.\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest powers of all common prime factors. Here, the common prime factor is \\(3\\), and the lowest power of \\(3\\) in both factorizations is \\(3^6\\).\n\nThus, the GCD is:\n\\[3^6 = 729.\\]\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of \\(6^3\\) and \\(3^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{729}\\)."
"To simplify $\\sqrt{32670}$, we can factorize the number under the square root:\n\n$$32670 = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 23$$\n\nNow, we can take the square root of each factor:\n\n$$\\sqrt{32670} = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 23} = \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{7} \\cdot \\sqrt{23}$$\n\nSince the square root of a perfect square is the number itself, we have:\n\n$$\\sqrt{32670} = 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sqrt{23} = 210 \\cdot \\sqrt{23}$$\n\nTherefore, the simplified form of $\\sqrt{32670}$ is:\n\n$$\\boxed{210\\sqrt{23}}$$\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $210\\sqrt{23}$."
