"To determine the total area of the shaded region, we need to identify the areas of the shaded triangles and sum them up.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the structure of the rectangle. The rectangle is divided into twelve 1 by 1 squares, forming a 3x4 grid. The diagonals divide the rectangle into four smaller triangles.\n\nThe shaded region consists of two triangles, each with a base of 1 unit and a height of 4 units. The area of one triangle is calculated as:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times 4 = 2 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nSince there are two such triangles, the total area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of shaded region} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\nThus, the total area of the shaded region is \\(\\boxed{4}\\) square units."
