"To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of \\(144\\) and \\(405\\), we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of zero.\n\n1. Divide \\(405\\) by \\(144\\) and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   405 \\div 144 = 3 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 3\n   \\]\n   So, \\(405 = 144 \\cdot 3 + 3\\).\n\n2. Now, divide \\(144\\) by \\(3\\) and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   144 \\div 3 = 48 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 0\n   \\]\n   So, \\(144 = 3 \\cdot 48 + 0\\).\n\nSince the remainder is now \\(0\\), the GCF is the last non-zero remainder, which is \\(3\\).\n\nTherefore, the greatest common factor of \\(144\\) and \\(405\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{x}{3} = 5\\), we first find a common denominator for the fractions on the left side. The least common multiple of 2 and 3 is 6. Therefore, we rewrite the equation with a common denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{x}{3} = \\frac{3x}{6} + \\frac{2x}{6} = \\frac{5x}{6}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5x}{6} = 5\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(x\\) by multiplying both sides of the equation by 6:\n\n\\[\n5x = 30\n\\]\n\nThen, we divide both sides by 5:\n\n\\[\nx = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]"
