"We know that $\\sin (\\pi \\cos x) = \\cos (\\pi \\sin x)$ implies that $\\pi \\cos x = \\pi \\sin x + 2k\\pi$ for some integer $k$. Simplifying, we get $\\cos x = \\sin x + 2k$, which is not possible for any real $x$. Therefore, there are no solutions to the equation.\n\nHowever, we can use the fact that $\\sin 2x = 2 \\sin x \\cos x$ to find the possible values of $\\sin 2x$. Since there are no solutions to the equation, the possible values of $\\sin 2x$ are $\\boxed{0}$."
"To find the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ that swaps the rows of a matrix, we need to find a matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ such that:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} c & d \\\\ a & b \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThis means that $\\mathbf{M}$ should be a matrix that, when multiplied by any $2 \\times 2$ matrix, swaps its rows. The only matrix that satisfies this property is the swap matrix, which is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{M} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the matrix $\\mathbf{M}$ that swaps the rows of a matrix is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]"
"We know that $\\cos \\theta = \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{1 + \\sin \\theta} + \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{1 - \\sin \\theta} = \\frac{\\sin \\theta}{(1 + \\sin \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta)} = \\frac{4}{7}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\\[\\frac{1}{1 + \\sin \\theta} + \\frac{1}{1 - \\sin \\theta} = \\frac{1 + \\sin \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta}{(1 + \\sin \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta)} = \\frac{1}{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta} = \\frac{1}{1 - \\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)^2} = \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{16}{49}} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{33}{49}} = \\frac{49}{33}.\\]\n\nSo, the answer is $\\boxed{\\frac{49}{33}}.$"
"We have that\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix} x_1 & y_1 & 1 \\\\ x_2 & y_2 & 1 \\\\ x_3 & y_3 & 1 \\end{vmatrix}^2 = x_1 y_2 - x_2 y_1 + x_2 y_3 - x_3 y_2 + x_1 y_3 - x_1 y_3 - x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_2 - x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_3 - x_1 y_2 + x_1 y_3 - x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_2 - x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_3 - x_1 y_2 + x_1 y_3 - x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_2 - x_2 y_1 + x_3 y_3 - x_1 y_2 = \\boxed{50}.\n\\]"
"We can simplify the right-hand side of the equation using the tangent addition formula:\n\n$$\\tan{19x^{\\circ}}=\\dfrac{\\cos{96^{\\circ}}+\\sin{96^{\\circ}}}{\\cos{96^{\\circ}}-\\sin{96^{\\circ}}}=\\tan{96^{\\circ}}.$$\n\nSince the tangent function is periodic with period $180^{\\circ}$, we have:\n\n$$\\tan{19x^{\\circ}}=\\tan{96^{\\circ}}.$$\n\nThis implies that:\n\n$$19x^{\\circ}=96^{\\circ}+180^{\\circ}k$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. Solving for $x$, we get:\n\n$$x=4+10k.$$\n\nThe smallest positive integer solution is $x=4$."
